# Pine Beetle Frontend Project Rules

## General Guidelines

- Use English for all code and documentation.
- Write concise, technical code with accurate examples.
- Use functional and declarative programming patterns; avoid classes.
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication.
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`).
- Follow existing project structure and naming conventions.

## TypeScript Rules

- Always declare the type of each variable and function (parameters and return value).
- Avoid using `any`; create necessary types instead.
- Use interface definitions for object shapes and component props.
- Prefer type unions over enums when possible.
- Use generic types for reusable components and functions.
- Define return types explicitly for functions, especially in Redux actions and reducers.
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`) operators.
- Leverage TypeScript's strict mode settings.

## SCSS/Sass Rules

- Use SCSS variables from `src/styles/colors.scss` for all color values.
- Follow BEM methodology for CSS class naming when appropriate.
- Use nested selectors sparingly (max 3 levels deep).
- Leverage SCSS mixins for repeated patterns (animations, transitions).
- Use `@import` statements at the top of files for dependencies.
- Follow the existing color variable naming convention (`$colorName` + `$shade`).
- Use semantic color names (e.g., `$headingColor`, `$backgroundColor`) over generic ones.
- Keep component-specific styles in their respective `style.scss` files.
- Use CSS custom properties for dynamic values when needed.

## React.js Rules

- Use functional components with hooks over class components.
- Follow the existing component structure: `component.js`, `index.js`, `style.scss`.
- Use PropTypes for prop validation (as seen in the project).
- Implement proper error boundaries for component error handling.
- Use React.memo() for performance optimization when appropriate.
- Follow the existing Redux pattern with actions, reducers, and thunks.
- Use descriptive component and function names.
- Implement proper cleanup in useEffect hooks (remove event listeners, clear timers).
- Use React Router patterns as established in the project.
- Follow the existing state management structure with Redux.

## Node.js & Build Tools Rules

- Follow the existing Webpack configuration patterns.
- Use Babel for JavaScript transpilation with the configured presets.
- Follow the existing ESLint configuration (Airbnb style guide).
- Use the established npm scripts for development and production builds.
- Follow the existing file structure for assets and components.
- Use environment variables for configuration (following the Dotenv pattern).
- Follow the existing PostCSS and Autoprefixer setup.
- Use the established Sass loader configuration with color imports.
- Follow the existing development server configuration with hot reloading.

## File Organization Rules

- Keep components in their respective directories with `component.js`, `index.js`, and `style.scss`.
- Use the existing screen structure for page-level components.
- Follow the established services pattern for API calls.
- Use the existing utils structure for helper functions.
- Follow the established constants organization.
- Keep assets organized by type (icons, images, animations).
- Use the existing state management structure (actions, reducers).

## Code Quality Rules

- Follow the existing ESLint configuration and fix all linting errors.
- Use meaningful commit messages following conventional commit format.
- Write self-documenting code with clear variable and function names.
- Avoid deep nesting in both JavaScript and SCSS.
- Use consistent indentation (2 spaces for JS, 4 spaces for SCSS as per project).
- Follow the existing import/export patterns.
- Use destructuring for props and state when appropriate.
